\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:introduction}

Let \(\K\) be an algebraically closed field of characteristic zero.  A
\emph{plane Keller pair} is a pair \(P,Q\in\K[x,y]\) for which
\[
 [P,Q]
 :=P_xQ_y-P_yQ_x
 \in\K^\times.
\]
The plane Jacobian conjecture asserts that every such pair defines a
polynomial automorphism of \(\A^2_{\K}\).  The problem originates with
Keller~\cite{Keller1939} and remains open.

One quantitative approach asks for the least possible degree of a
counterexample.  We use the ordered degree pair
\((\deg P,\deg Q)\) and call
\[
 d(P,Q):=\max\{\deg P,\deg Q\}
\]
the \emph{maximum component degree}.  Moh's 1983 analysis established the
classical lower-bound frontier at \(100\)~\cite{Moh1983}.  A sequence of
Newton-polygon and corner algorithms subsequently made the remaining small
configurations explicit
\cite{Guccione2017Shape,Guccione2017Algorithms,Guccione2022Degree}.

The result from which we start is especially sharp.  Guccione, Guccione,
Horruitiner, and Valqui proved that a counterexample either has maximum
component degree at least \(125\), or has ordered degree pair
\((72,108)\) or \((108,72)\)~\cite[Theorem~2.1]{Guccione2022Degree}.
They then reduced the unresolved corner for \((72,108)\) to two explicit
Laurent-polynomial support configurations
\cite[Proposition~4.3]{Guccione2022Degree}.  The present paper closes those
two configurations.

Two contemporaneous public records concern this same frontier.  A
MathOverflow answer announces the bound (125) by a computer-assisted
elimination and describes its write-up as in preparation
\cite{MathOverflow125}.  Santib\'a\~nez-Leal reports exact-arithmetic machine
evidence at the \((72,108)\) frontier, while explicitly leaving the
simultaneous certificate needed for closure open
\cite{SantibanezLeal2026}.  The proof below is self-contained relative to
the cited Guccione--Guccione--Horruitiner--Valqui reduction and does not use
either contemporaneous record.

\begin{theorem}
\label{thm:main}
Let \(\K\) be an algebraically closed field of characteristic zero, and let
\((P,Q)\in\K[x,y]^2\) satisfy \([P,Q]\in\K^\times\).  If the map
\((P,Q):\A^2_{\K}\to\A^2_{\K}\) is not a polynomial automorphism, then
\[
 \max\{\deg P,\deg Q\}\geq125.
\]
\end{theorem}

The proof is finite but not a black-box search.  Put \(t=xy^2\).  In each of
the two residual supports, the Laurent pair decomposes into finitely many
one-variable slices.  Direct differentiation turns \([P,Q]=x^2\) into a
triangular sequence of polynomial ordinary differential equations.  The
lowest layer defines a degree-\(21\) Belyi map.  An exact Hurwitz count gives
five normalized solutions, represented by the conjugates of a point over
\[
 \Q(\theta),\qquad
 \theta^5-\theta^4-9\theta^3+\theta^2+24\theta-18=0.
\]
For the smaller support, three further layers give seven compatibility
conditions in two parameters.  Two quartic eliminants have an exact Bézout
identity.  For the support with a positive vertical side, the successive
layers leave five parameters and nine compatibility polynomials.  An exact
square relation forces a hyperplane; on it, all remaining conditions are
linear in one parameter.  The closed and open coefficient charts are then
excluded by two exact certificates.  The open-chart structure is visible
before the terminal calculation: its four special-fibre equations are four
pairwise nonproportional affine-linear factors times one common quadratic.
Removing that common special-fibre part leaves four smaller generators.  The
first three define a 52-dimensional quotient; lucky reduction modulo 67
preserves its standard monomial basis and makes multiplication by the fourth
full rank, so the fourth generator is already a unit in characteristic zero.

The machine-assisted part is therefore exact arithmetic over
\(\Q(\theta)\) and \(\mathbb F_{67^5}\).  The finite field is used only to
prove nonvanishing of a specified characteristic-zero multiplication
determinant after a checked lucky specialization; no modular unit ideal is
lifted back heuristically.  The supplement fixes the coefficient fields,
input polynomials, monomial order, and terminal certificates, and it provides
independent checks of every displayed layer equation.

\begin{corollary}
\label{cor:exceptional-pair}
No plane Keller counterexample has degree pair \((72,108)\) or
\((108,72)\).
\end{corollary}

Theorem~\ref{thm:main} is a degree bound, not a resolution of the Jacobian
conjecture.  It says nothing about a hypothetical counterexample whose
maximum component degree is at least \(125\).

\paragraph{Organization.}
Section~\ref{sec:small-degree} records the precise external reduction and
the two residual supports.  Section~\ref{sec:layers} derives the universal
one-variable layer formula.  Section~\ref{sec:first-layer} proves the
exhaustive quintic first-layer classification.
Sections~\ref{sec:case-ii} and~\ref{sec:case-i} exclude the two support
cases.  Section~\ref{sec:certification} specifies the exact certificate and
the boundary of the result.

