\section{The exhaustive first layer}
\label{sec:first-layer}

The lowest equation is common to both cases.  Write
\[
 A(t)=t\,a(t),\qquad B(t)=t^2b(t),
\]
where \(\deg a=7\), \(\deg b=10\), and the constant and leading
coefficients are nonzero.  Reciprocal component rescaling makes
\(a(0)=b(0)=1\).  Equation~\eqref{eq:E0} becomes
\[
 ab+t(2ab'-3a'b)=1.
\tag{4.1}\label{eq:first-layer}
\]
If
\[
 a=\sum_{i=0}^7a_it^i,\qquad
 b=\sum_{j=0}^{10}b_jt^j,
\]
then the coefficient of \(t^n\) in the left side of
\eqref{eq:first-layer} is
\[
 \sum_{i+j=n}(1+2n-5i)a_ib_j.
\tag{4.2}\label{eq:first-recurrence}
\]

For \(1\le n\le10\), equation
\eqref{eq:first-recurrence} solves \(b_n\) triangularly from
\(a_1,\ldots,a_{\min(n,7)}\).  The remaining nontrivial equations occur
in degrees \(11,\ldots,16\); the coefficient in degree \(17\) vanishes
identically.

\begin{lemma}[First-layer scaling charts]
\label{lem:first-scaling}
The nondegenerate first layer is covered, without loss, by the two charts
\[
 a_1=1,\quad a_7\ne0,
 \qquad\text{and}\qquad
 a_1=0,\quad a_7=1.
\tag{4.3}\label{eq:first-charts}
\]
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
The endpoint vertices give \(a_7\ne0\).  For \(\gamma\in\K^\times\), set
\[
 \widetilde P(x,y)=\gamma^{-1}P(\gamma x,y),\qquad
 \widetilde Q(x,y)=\gamma^{-2}Q(\gamma x,y).
\]
The chain rule gives
\([\widetilde P,\widetilde Q]=x^2\), and the lowest slices become
\(t\,a(\gamma t)\) and \(t^2b(\gamma t)\).  Thus \(a_0=b_0=1\) is
preserved and \(a_i\) is multiplied by \(\gamma^i\).  If \(a_1\ne0\),
choose \(\gamma=a_1^{-1}\).  If \(a_1=0\), choose a seventh root of
\(a_7^{-1}\), which exists because the ground field is algebraically
closed.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[The associated Belyi cover]
\label{lem:first-layer-belyi}
Every nondegenerate solution of \eqref{eq:first-layer} determines a
normalized degree-\(21\) cover of \(\mathbb P^1\), with ordered branch
values \(0,\infty,1\), and passport
\[
 \mu_0=(2^{10},1),\qquad
 \mu_\infty=(3^7),\qquad
 \mu_1=(17,1^4).
\tag{4.4}\label{eq:first-passport}
\]
Two solutions with \(a_1=1\) determine isomorphic covers if and only if
they are equal.  Moreover, the property \(a_1=0\) is invariant under an
isomorphism of the associated covers.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Set
\[
 R(t)=\frac{t b(t)^2}{a(t)^3}.
\]
Equation~\eqref{eq:first-layer} gives the exact derivative identity
\[
 R'(t)=\frac{b(t)}{a(t)^4}.
\tag{4.5}\label{eq:first-derivative}
\]
The polynomials \(a\) and \(b\) are coprime: a common zero would make the
left side of \eqref{eq:first-layer} vanish.  At a zero of either polynomial,
the same equation shows that the zero is simple.  Since
\(a(0)=b(0)=1\), the zero divisor of \(R\) has type \((2^{10},1)\), and
its pole divisor has type \((3^7)\).

The numerator and denominator of \(R\) both have degree \(21\), so
\(R(\infty)=:\lambda\in\K^\times\).  From
\eqref{eq:first-derivative}, in the local coordinate \(z=t^{-1}\) one has
\(dR/dz\) of order \(16\).  Thus the ramification index of \(R\) at
infinity is \(17\).  The ramification contributions already found are
\[
 10+7(3-1)+(17-1)=40=2\cdot21-2.
\]
Riemann--Hurwitz therefore leaves no further critical point, and the four
remaining points above \(\lambda\) are unramified.  After scaling the target
so that \(\lambda=1\), this is \eqref{eq:first-passport}.

The three cycle types in \eqref{eq:first-passport} are distinct, so an
isomorphism between two normalized covers preserves the three ordered branch
values.  The point \(t=0\) is the unique unramified point above the first
branch value, and \(t=\infty\) is the unique point of ramification index
\(17\).  Consequently the source automorphism is, in these coordinates,
\(t\mapsto\gamma t\).  If \(R_i/\lambda_i\) are two normalized maps and
\(R_2(t)/\lambda_2=R_1(\gamma t)/\lambda_1\), their zero and pole divisors,
together with \(a_i(0)=b_i(0)=1\), give
\[
 a_2(t)=a_1(\gamma t),\qquad b_2(t)=b_1(\gamma t).
\]
Hence \((a_2)_1=\gamma(a_1)_1\).  The last two assertions follow: in the
chart \(a_1=1\), one has \(\gamma=1\), while vanishing of \(a_1\) is
preserved by every residual scaling.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Exact Hurwitz count]
\label{lem:first-hurwitz}
There are exactly five isomorphism classes of complex covers with passport
\eqref{eq:first-passport}.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
We use branch cycles \((\sigma_0,\sigma_\infty,\sigma_1)\) with
\(\sigma_0\sigma_\infty\sigma_1=1\), and simultaneous conjugacy in
\(S_{21}\).  For a cycle type \(\mu=(1^{m_1}2^{m_2}\cdots)\), put
\(z_\mu=\prod_i i^{m_i}m_i!\).  The Frobenius class-algebra formula gives
the weighted number of simultaneous-conjugacy classes of branch-cycle
triples, each orbit weighted by the reciprocal of its common-centralizer
order, as~\cite{JamesKerber1981,LandoZvonkin2004}
\[
 H=\frac{21!}{z_{\mu_0}z_{\mu_\infty}z_{\mu_1}}
 \sum_{\rho\vdash21}
 \frac{\chi^\rho(\mu_0)\chi^\rho(\mu_\infty)
       \chi^\rho(\mu_1)}{f^\rho},
\tag{4.6}\label{eq:hurwitz-formula}
\]
where \(f^\rho=\chi^\rho(1^{21})\).  Only nineteen summands are nonzero.
Because all three classes consist of even permutations, conjugate
partitions give equal summands.  The following table lists one member of
each conjugate pair; \(m_\rho\) is two, except for the self-conjugate last
row.
\[
\begin{array}{c|r|r|r|r|r|c}
\rho&m_\rho&\chi_0&\chi_\infty&\chi_1&f^\rho&
 \chi_0\chi_\infty\chi_1/f^\rho\\ \hline
(21)&2&1&1&1&1&1\\
(19,1^2)&2&-10&1&3&190&-3/19\\
(17,4)&2&35&-7&-3&4655&3/19\\
(15,5,1)&2&-120&-21&1&175560&3/209\\
(15,3^2)&2&-315&42&2&181545&-36/247\\
(15,2^3)&2&280&35&-1&186200&-1/19\\
(13,3,2^2,1)&2&-420&21&3&7987980&-9/2717\\
(11,4,2,1^4)&2&1680&35&3&53253200&9/2717\\
(9,5,1^7)&2&2100&-105&1&28528500&-21/2717\\
(9,3^2,1^6)&1&4032&140&2&59643584&360/19019
\end{array}
\tag{4.7}\label{eq:hurwitz-table}
\]
Murnaghan--Nakayama and the hook-length formula reproduce every entry in
\eqref{eq:hurwitz-table}.  Summing with the multiplicities shown gives
\[
 \sum_{\rho\vdash21}
 \frac{\chi^\rho(\mu_0)\chi^\rho(\mu_\infty)
       \chi^\rho(\mu_1)}{f^\rho}
 =\frac{31104}{19019}.
\]
Since
\[
 z_{\mu_0}=2^{10}10!,\qquad
 z_{\mu_\infty}=3^7 7!,\qquad
 z_{\mu_1}=17\cdot4!,
\]
equation~\eqref{eq:hurwitz-formula} gives \(H=5\).

It remains to remove the weighting and the possible disconnected triples.
An orbit of the generated permutation group which does not meet the support
of the unique \(17\)-cycle must lie among the four fixed points of
\(\sigma_1\).  Since \(\sigma_\infty\) is a product of \(3\)-cycles, the
only possible nonempty such orbit has size three.  On it \(\sigma_1\) is the
identity, while \(\sigma_0\) has type \((2,1)\) (it has only one fixed point
on all twenty-one letters) and \(\sigma_\infty\) has type \((3)\).  Their
product cannot be the identity.
Thus every triple is transitive.

The common centralizer of a transitive triple acts semiregularly.  It must
preserve both the support of the unique \(17\)-cycle and its four-point
fixed set.  The order of a semiregular centralizer therefore divides both
\(17\) and \(4\), and is one.  Every weight is consequently one.  The
Riemann existence theorem now identifies the five branch-cycle orbits with
five isomorphism classes of complex covers~\cite{LandoZvonkin2004}.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[First-layer certificate]
\label{prop:first-layer-certificate}
The second chart in \eqref{eq:first-charts} is empty.  The geometric points
of the first chart are precisely the five conjugates of one point over
\[
 K=\Q(\theta),\qquad
 f(\theta)=0,
\tag{4.8}\label{eq:quintic-field}
\]
where
\[
 f(T)=T^5-T^4-9T^3+T^2+24T-18.
\tag{4.9}\label{eq:quintic}
\]
The coefficients \(a_2,\ldots,a_7\) of this point are the
explicit elements of the power basis
\(1,\theta,\ldots,\theta^4\) recorded in the supplement; the
coefficients \(b_1,\ldots,b_{10}\) are then given by
\eqref{eq:first-recurrence}.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
The six tail equations are obtained by substituting the triangular
expressions for \(b_1,\ldots,b_{10}\) in
\eqref{eq:first-recurrence}.  The supplement gives elements
\(\alpha_2,\ldots,\alpha_7\in K\).  Exact substitution annihilates all six
tail equations, and the recurrence defines \(b_1,\ldots,b_{10}\); direct
coefficient expansion verifies all eighteen coefficients of
\eqref{eq:first-layer} and the required nonzero endpoints.  The displayed
element \(\alpha_2\) is not rational.

It remains to verify that \(f\) is irreducible over \(\Q\).  Its squarefree
factorizations are
\[
\begin{aligned}
 f&=(T^2-2)(T^3-T^2-2T-1)\pmod 5,\\
 f&=(T+9)(T^4-10T^3-11T^2+8T-2)\pmod {23},
\end{aligned}
\]
and the displayed factors are irreducible in their respective finite
fields.  Indeed, \(T^2-2\) has no root modulo \(5\), and the values of the
displayed cubic at \(0,1,2,3,4\) are respectively
\(4,2,4,1,4\).  For the quartic \(g\) modulo \(23\), repeated squaring gives
\[
 T^{23^2}-T\equiv4T^3-8T^2+6T+4=:r\pmod g,
\]
and a direct Euclidean identity is
\[
 (-3T^2+11T+11)g+(-5T^3-3T^2-5T)r=1\pmod {23}.
\]
Thus \(g\) has no factor of degree one or two and is irreducible.  A proper
rational factor degree would have to be a proper subset sum in both
factorizations, which is impossible.  Hence \([K:\Q]=5\); since the degree
is prime and \(\alpha_2\notin\Q\), the five embeddings of \(K\) give five
distinct solutions in the chart \(a_1=1\).

By Lemmas~\ref{lem:first-layer-belyi} and \ref{lem:first-hurwitz}, these
solutions already account for all five isomorphism classes of covers with
passport \eqref{eq:first-passport}.  An additional solution in the first
chart would give a sixth class.  A solution with \(a_1=0\) cannot be
isomorphic to any of the five displayed covers, because vanishing of
\(a_1\) is invariant under the residual source scaling.  The second chart
is therefore empty as well.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
\label{cor:first-layer-base-change}
Over every algebraically closed field of characteristic zero, every
normalized nondegenerate first layer is obtained by a base change of
\eqref{eq:quintic-field}.  Consequently it suffices to exclude the later
layers over \(K=\Q(\theta)\).
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}
The defining equations have rational coefficients.  Any putative solution
is defined over a finitely generated extension of \(\Q\), which embeds in
\(\mathbb C\).  Proposition~\ref{prop:first-layer-certificate} classifies the
resulting complex point as one of the five conjugates of the displayed
\(K\)-point.  Thus no additional geometric solution appears after extension
to an arbitrary algebraically closed characteristic-zero field.
\end{proof}

