\section{Exclusion of Case II}
\label{sec:case-ii}

We now work over the exact first-layer field
\[
 K=\Q(\theta),\qquad
 \theta^5-\theta^4-9\theta^3+\theta^2+24\theta-18=0.
\]
The coefficients of \(A\) and \(B\) are those fixed by
Proposition~\ref{prop:first-layer-certificate}.  The top coefficients of
the remaining slices are fixed by \eqref{eq:top-normalization}.

\subsection{The linear layers}

In equation~\eqref{eq:E1}, take as unknowns
\[
 C_1,\ldots,C_7,\qquad E_2,\ldots,E_{11},
\]
with \(C_8=2A_8\) and \(E_{12}=3B_{12}\).  Equating
\(t\)-coefficients gives a \(K\)-linear system of rank \(16\) in \(17\)
unknowns.  We write its single parameter as \(u\).

Equation~\eqref{eq:E2} is then linear in
\[
 D_0,\ldots,D_7,\qquad F_1,\ldots,F_{11},
\]
with \(D_8=A_8\) and \(F_{12}=3B_{12}\).  Its rank is \(17\) in \(19\)
unknowns.  One kernel vector changes only \(D_0\), which never occurs
after differentiation and is therefore irrelevant.  We set \(D_0=0\)
and denote the essential parameter by \(w\).

Finally, \eqref{eq:E3} is linear in \(G_1,\ldots,G_{11}\), with
\(G_{12}=B_{12}\).  The free constant \(G_0\) occurs in neither
\eqref{eq:E3} nor \eqref{eq:E4} and is left arbitrary.  The coefficient
matrix has rank \(11\), and its left kernel gives seven nonzero compatibility
polynomials
\[
 q_1(u,w),\ldots,q_7(u,w)\in K[u,w].
\]
The entire calculation is summarized by
\[
\begin{array}{c|ccc}
\text{equation}&\text{unknowns}&\text{rank}&\text{outcome}\\ \hline
\mathrm{E1}&17&16&u\\
\mathrm{E2}&19&17&D_0\text{ and }w\\
\mathrm{E3}&11&11&7\text{ compatibility equations}.
\end{array}
\tag{5.1}\label{eq:case-II-ranks}
\]

\begin{lemma}
\label{lem:case-II-linear}
The coefficient expressions obtained above satisfy
\eqref{eq:E1} and \eqref{eq:E2} identically in \(K[u,w][t]\), and the
system \eqref{eq:E3} has a solution only if all seven \(q_i\) vanish.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Row reduction is performed over the field \(K\), with no division by a
polynomial in \(u\) or \(w\).  Substitution of the resulting coefficient
vectors gives zero in every coefficient of \eqref{eq:E1} and
\eqref{eq:E2}.  The compatibility conditions for \eqref{eq:E3} are the
pairings of its right-hand side with a basis of the exact left kernel.
Thus their vanishing is necessary for solvability, including at special
parameter values.
\end{proof}

\subsection{The terminal Bézout identity}

Every compatibility polynomial has support contained in
\[
 \{1,u,u^2,u^3,w,uw\}.
\tag{5.2}\label{eq:q-support}
\]
Write
\[
 q_i(u,w)=A_i(u)w+B_i(u).
\]
From the first three conditions form
\[
\begin{aligned}
 h_{12}(u)&=A_1(u)B_2(u)-A_2(u)B_1(u),\\
 h_{13}(u)&=A_1(u)B_3(u)-A_3(u)B_1(u).
\end{aligned}
\tag{5.3}\label{eq:h12-h13}
\]
Both polynomials have degree four.

\begin{proposition}[Case-II certificate]
\label{prop:case-II-certificate}
There are cubic polynomials \(S,T\in K[u]\) such that
\[
 S(u)h_{12}(u)+T(u)h_{13}(u)=1.
\tag{5.4}\label{eq:case-II-bezout}
\]
Consequently Case II has no solution.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
The power-basis coefficient vectors of \(h_{12},h_{13},S,T\) are fixed in
the supplementary certificate.  Multiplication in
\(\Q[\theta,u]\), followed by reduction by the monic polynomial
\eqref{eq:quintic}, verifies \eqref{eq:case-II-bezout} coefficient by
coefficient.

If all \(q_i\) vanished at a pair \((u,w)\), then eliminating \(w\) from
\((q_1,q_2)\) and from \((q_1,q_3)\) would give
\(h_{12}(u)=h_{13}(u)=0\).  This contradicts
\eqref{eq:case-II-bezout}.  Thus equation~\eqref{eq:E3} cannot be solved.
Equation~\eqref{eq:E4} is not needed.
\end{proof}

The certificate uses the exhaustive quintic coordinate algebra, not one
chosen numerical root of \eqref{eq:quintic}.  It therefore excludes all
five conjugate first-layer points simultaneously and remains valid after
base change to an algebraic closure.

