\section{Exclusion of Case I}
\label{sec:case-i}

Case I contains the positive vertical-side slices absent from Case II.
We retain the layer notation \eqref{eq:case-I-slices} and the exact
coefficient field \(K=\Q(\theta)\).  At total slice \(m\), equation
\(L_m=0\) from \eqref{eq:Lm} is linear in the new coefficient vectors
of \(p_m\) and \(q_m\).  All nonlinear terms involve only earlier
slices.

The certified first-layer point has \(a_6a_7\ne0\).  Before propagating
the later layers, we make one further lossless source rescaling from
Lemma~\ref{lem:first-scaling}.  Namely, put
\[
 \gamma_*=\frac{a_6}{a_7}
 \quad\hbox{and replace}\quad
 a(t),b(t)\ \hbox{by}\ a(\gamma_*t),b(\gamma_*t).
\tag{6.1}\label{eq:case-I-height-scaling}
\]
Equation~\eqref{eq:first-layer} is invariant under this substitution, and
the new coefficients satisfy
\[
 a_6\gamma_*^6=a_7\gamma_*^7.
\]
Thus we may, and do, assume \(a_6=a_7\).  This changes neither Newton
support nor the bracket equation; it only lowers the heights of the exact
number-field coefficients used below.

\subsection{Exact layer propagation}

The top normalization gives
\[
\begin{aligned}
 p_{1,8}&=2A_8,&p_{2,8}&=A_8,\\
 q_{1,12}&=3B_{12},&
 q_{2,12}&=3B_{12},&
 q_{3,12}&=B_{12}.
\end{aligned}
\tag{6.2}\label{eq:case-I-top}
\]
Exact left-kernel propagation over \(K\) gives the following profile.
\[
\begin{array}{c|rrrrr}
 m&\text{rows}&\text{unknowns}&\text{rank}&\text{nullity}
   &\text{new conditions}\\ \hline
 1&17&17&16&1&0\\
 2&18&19&17&2&0\\
 3&18&20&17&3&0\\
 4&19&19&18&1&0\\
 5&18&17&17&0&1\\
 6&18&15&15&0&3\\
 7&18&13&13&0&5
\end{array}
\tag{6.3}\label{eq:case-I-profile}
\]
At \(m=2\), one null direction changes only the irrelevant constant
\(p_{2,0}\); at \(m=3\), one changes only \(q_{3,0}\).  Removing these
directions, the essential parameters introduced through \(m=4\) are
\[
 u,w,r,s,v.
\]
The stages \(m=5,6,7\) yield nine compatibility polynomials
\[
 F_1,\ldots,F_9\in K[u,w,r,s,v].
\tag{6.4}\label{eq:case-I-ideal}
\]
Their degrees and support sizes are
\[
\begin{array}{c|c|c}
i&\deg F_i&\#\supp(F_i)\\ \hline
1&5&22\\
2&6&35\\
3&6&35\\
4&5&23\\
5&7&52\\
6&7&52\\
7&7&52\\
8&6&45\\
9&5&23.
\end{array}
\tag{6.5}\label{eq:case-I-condition-profile}
\]

\begin{lemma}
\label{lem:case-I-necessity}
If a Case-I pair exists, then the nine polynomials in
\eqref{eq:case-I-ideal} have a common zero over an algebraic closure of
\(K\).
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
At each stage, choose pivot rows and columns from the coefficient matrix
over \(K\).  Solving the corresponding square nonsingular minor introduces
only the kernel parameters listed above.  Pairing the right-hand side with
the full left kernel gives all compatibility equations, without division
by a parameter polynomial.  Hence every solution of the complete layer
system specializes the five parameters to a common zero of all nine
\(F_i\).
\end{proof}

\subsection{A square relation and an exhaustive two-chart certificate}

Let
\[
 \mathcal R
 =\Q[\theta,u,w,r,s,v]\big/
   (\theta^5-\theta^4-9\theta^3+\theta^2+24\theta-18).
\]
The supplement serializes the nine \(F_i\) in the power basis for
\(\theta\), using degree-reverse-lexicographic order on
\((u,w,r,s,v)\).  The direct unit-ideal calculation becomes much smaller
after the following exact relation is extracted from these polynomials.

\begin{lemma}[Square and hyperplane reduction]
\label{lem:case-I-square}
There are \(c,\alpha,\lambda_3,\lambda_6\in K\), with \(c\ne0\), such
that
\[
 F_9-\frac49F_1
 =c\left(u-\frac23w+\frac13s+\alpha\right)^2.
\tag{6.6}\label{eq:case-I-square}
\]
Put
\[
 \widehat F_3=F_3-\lambda_3F_2,
 \qquad
 \widehat F_6=F_6-\lambda_6F_5.
\]
After substituting \(s=-3u+2w-3\alpha\), define
\[
 (H_1,\ldots,H_5)
 =\left(F_1,F_2,\widehat F_3,F_5,\widehat F_6\right)
   \big|_{s=-3u+2w-3\alpha}.
\tag{6.7}\label{eq:case-I-H}
\]
The substitution removes \(s\), so the five resulting polynomials belong to
\(K[u,w,r,v]\).
The degree and support profiles of the \(H_i\) are
\[
 (5,18),\ (6,27),\ (5,26),\ (7,38),\ (6,37).
\tag{6.8}\label{eq:case-I-H-profile}
\]
Each \(H_i\) is linear in \(v\); write
\[
 H_i=A_i(u,w,r)v+B_i(u,w,r).
\tag{6.9}\label{eq:case-I-linear-v}
\]
For some \(a\in K^\times\) and \(u_0\in K\), one has
\[
 A_1=a(u-u_0).
\tag{6.10}\label{eq:case-I-A1}
\]
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
All assertions are coefficient identities in the power basis
\(1,\theta,\ldots,\theta^4\).  The supplementary generator reconstructs
the nine \(F_i\) from the layer recurrence, performs the two scalar row
operations, and expands both sides of \eqref{eq:case-I-square}.  Reduction
of the coefficients by \eqref{eq:quintic} gives zero.  It then makes the
displayed substitution and checks \eqref{eq:case-I-H-profile}--
\eqref{eq:case-I-A1} term by term.  The serialized nonzero coefficients
\(c\) and \(a\) are elements of the field \(K\), so neither can vanish
after the calculation is base-changed to an algebraic closure.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[Case-I certificate]
\label{prop:case-I-certificate}
The ideal generated by \(F_1,\ldots,F_9\) in
\(K[u,w,r,s,v]\) is the unit ideal.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
By \eqref{eq:case-I-square}, a common zero of the nine \(F_i\) must lie on
the hyperplane used in \eqref{eq:case-I-H}.  Every such zero is therefore a
common zero of \(H_1,\ldots,H_5\).  We cover this hyperplane by the closed
chart \(A_1=0\) and the open chart \(A_1\ne0\).

On the closed chart, \eqref{eq:case-I-A1} gives \(u=u_0\).  Set
\[
 J_i(w,r,v)=H_i(u_0,w,r,v)\qquad(1\le i\le5).
\]
Their degree and support profiles are
\[
 (2,5),\ (3,9),\ (3,9),\ (3,11),\ (3,11).
\]
A direct lift is performed in \(K[w,r,v]\), with degree-reverse-lexicographic
order on \((w,r,v)\).  It gives \(C_i\in K[w,r,v]\) with
\[
 \sum_{i=1}^5 C_iJ_i=1.
\tag{6.11}\label{eq:case-I-closed-bezout}
\]
Thus the closed chart is empty.

For the open chart define, for \(2\le i\le5\),
\[
 R_i=A_1B_i-A_iB_1\in K[u,w,r].
\tag{6.12}\label{eq:case-I-resultants}
\]
Their degree and support profiles are
\[
 (7,31),\ (7,31),\ (8,40),\ (8,40).
\]
There are \(\rho_3,\rho_5\in K\) for which the scalar row operations
\[
 S_2=R_2,\quad S_3=R_3-\rho_3R_2,\quad
 S_4=R_4,\quad S_5=R_5-\rho_5R_4
\]
give the smaller degree and support profiles
\[
 (7,31),\ (6,30),\ (8,40),\ (7,39).
\]
Finally set \(\xi=u-u_0\) and
\(\widetilde S_i(\xi,w,r)=S_i(\xi+u_0,w,r)\).  Their profiles are
\[
 (7,30),\ (6,29),\ (8,40),\ (7,39).
\]
We now replace each \(\widetilde S_i\) by the nonzero scalar multiple listed
in the supplement and retain the same notation.  Exact factorization on the
special fibre reveals the common structure
\[
 \widetilde S_i(0,w,r)=\ell_i(w,r)g(w,r)
 \qquad(2\le i\le5),
\tag{6.13}\label{eq:case-I-common-quadratic}
\]
where \(g\) is a five-term quadratic and the four \(\ell_i\) are affine
linear.  For every pair, at least one coefficient minor is nonzero, so the
four affine-linear forms are pairwise nonproportional.  Their degree and
support-size profiles are
\[
 (\deg g,\#\supp g)=(2,5),\qquad
 (\deg\ell_i,\#\supp\ell_i)=(1,2),(1,2),(1,3),(1,3).
\]
The first two factors have the form
\[
 \ell_2=a_2'w+b_2',\qquad
 \ell_3=a_3'w+b_3',\qquad
 \delta=a_3'b_2'-a_2'b_3'\ne0.
\tag{6.14}\label{eq:case-I-linear-determinant}
\]
Define
\[
 G=\frac{a_3'\widetilde S_2-a_2'\widetilde S_3}{\delta}.
\tag{6.15}\label{eq:case-I-G}
\]
Then \(G(0,w,r)=g(w,r)\).  Consequently each numerator below is divisible
by \(\xi\) in the polynomial ring:
\[
 V_i=\frac{\widetilde S_i-\ell_iG}{\xi}
 \in K[\xi,w,r]\qquad(2\le i\le5).
\tag{6.16}\label{eq:case-I-residuals}
\]
Direct expansion verifies all four division identities; moreover
\(a_3'V_2=a_2'V_3\).  The profiles of the four retained residual
generators are
\[
 (\deg G,\#\supp G)=(7,28),\quad
 (\deg V_2,\#\supp V_2)=(6,35),
\]
\[
 (\deg V_4,\#\supp V_4)=(7,47),\quad
 (\deg V_5,\#\supp V_5)=(6,46).
\tag{6.17}\label{eq:case-I-residual-profile}
\]
Let \(T_1,T_2,T_3,T_4\) be the four respective nonzero scalar multiples
of \(G,V_2,V_4,V_5\) recorded in the supplementary table, and put
\[
 I_0=(T_1,T_2,T_3),\qquad
 A=K[w,r,\xi]/I_0.
\tag{6.18}\label{eq:case-I-core-quotient}
\]
For degree reverse lexicographic order on \((w,r,\xi)\), the reduced
standard basis of \(I_0\) has 23 elements, and its 52 standard monomials
form a \(K\)-basis \(\mathcal B\) of \(A\).

We certify that \(T_4\) is a unit in \(A\) by one good reduction.  Write
\(f(t)\) for the quintic in \eqref{eq:quintic}, and \(\bar f\) for its
reduction modulo 67.  Exact Frobenius arithmetic gives
