\section{Certification and scope}
\label{sec:certification}

This section records what the finite computation proves and how it can be
checked without trusting a numerical approximation or a machine-local
state.

\subsection{Exact arithmetic contract}

There are three certificate layers.

\begin{enumerate}
\item The first-layer inputs are the six coefficients of degrees
  \(11,\ldots,16\) in \eqref{eq:first-recurrence}, together with one
  endpoint condition.  Lemma~\ref{lem:first-layer-belyi} turns every
  solution into a Belyi cover; the exact character table
  \eqref{eq:hurwitz-table} certifies that there are only five cover classes.
  Substitution of the quintic representation verifies every coefficient of
  \eqref{eq:first-layer} and supplies five distinct normalized solutions.
\item For Case II, all linear systems are reduced over the exact field
  \(K=\Q(\theta)\).  The final object is the polynomial identity
  \eqref{eq:case-II-bezout}; its verification consists only of polynomial
  multiplication and remainder modulo the monic quintic
  \eqref{eq:quintic}.
\item For Case I, the nine inputs are regenerated from the universal
  layer formula \eqref{eq:Lm}.  Coefficient expansion verifies the square
  identity \eqref{eq:case-I-square}, the hyperplane substitution, and the
  linearity assertion \eqref{eq:case-I-linear-v}.  Direct lifting over
  \(K\) gives the closed-chart column in
  \eqref{eq:case-I-closed-bezout}.  On the open chart, exact coefficient
  arithmetic verifies the common-quadratic factorizations
  \eqref{eq:case-I-common-quadratic}, the four divisions
  \eqref{eq:case-I-residuals}, and the 23-element, 52-dimensional core in
  \eqref{eq:case-I-core-quotient}.  Lucky reduction modulo 67 preserves its
  52 standard monomials, and multiplication by the fourth generator has
  exact rank 52.  This proves the characteristic-zero unit membership
  \eqref{eq:case-I-structural-unit}.
\end{enumerate}

The computations use rational, algebraic-number, and finite-field arithmetic
exactly.  A modularly reconstructed candidate basis equal to \(\{1\}\) is
not by itself treated as a proof: verifying that the input generators reduce
by a candidate basis does not establish reverse characteristic-zero ideal
containment.  The use of \(\mathbb F_{67^5}\) above is different.  The
program first computes the characteristic-zero core, checks 67-integrality,
and verifies that its initial monomial ideal survives specialization.  The
rank-52 calculation therefore reduces a specified characteristic-zero
multiplication determinant.  A nonzero reduction proves that determinant
nonzero in characteristic zero, so the argument is a deterministic
nonvanishing certificate rather than a modular inference of a unit ideal.

The standard-basis conventions and modular acceleration follow the
algorithms described by Arnold~\cite{Arnold2003} and implemented in
Singular~\cite{Singular}.  The logical verification of the final
certificates is independent of any probabilistic claim about good primes.

\subsection{Reproducibility}

The supplementary archive contains:
\begin{itemize}
\item the primitive polynomial and the power-basis coordinates of the
  first-layer point;
\item the exact Case-II compatibility and Bézout coefficient vectors;
\item human-readable sparse power-basis tables for the nine Case-I
  compatibility polynomials, the exact square and two-chart derivation, the
  common quadratic, its four affine-linear cofactors, and both terminal
  ideals;
\item a self-checking program for the closed characteristic-zero unit
  identity and the open lucky-reduction full-rank certificate, together with
  optional direct characteristic-zero standard-basis and lifting replays;
\item commands, expected invariant output, software versions, and SHA-256
  checksums.
\end{itemize}
The source inputs contain no floating-point constants.  Every success marker
is printed only after the corresponding polynomial remainder, standard
monomial comparison, or matrix rank has been checked exactly.  The manuscript
itself uses no machine-local file path.

\subsection{What the theorem does not say}

Theorem~\ref{thm:main} closes the only ordered degree pair left below
\(125\) by Theorem~\ref{thm:GGHV-small}.  It is unconditional once the
cited reduction and the exact certificates in the supplement are accepted.
It does not give:
\begin{itemize}
\item a classification of Keller pairs of degree at least \(125\);
\item a reduction of arbitrary degree to the \((8,28)\) corner;
\item an upper bound on the degree of a counterexample; or
\item a proof of the plane Jacobian conjecture.
\end{itemize}
Thus the result advances the quantitative frontier from \(108\) to \(125\)
while leaving the arbitrary-degree problem open.

